namespace Broiler.VM.Architecture.Tests;

/// <summary>
/// The exact text of every group J register row, held here so that a row cannot claim more than
/// the rules deliver.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para>
/// The register row is the only place a rule's honest limits are written down in prose, and
/// nothing else in the suite reads that prose: <c>RuleRegisterTests</c> asserts only that the
/// fields are non-empty. The version of this check before this one asserted that a row CONTAINED a
/// handful of required phrases, which holds a row to stating its limits and does not hold it to
/// stating nothing else. A sentence claiming that CI compares every human line against the parent
/// commit was appended to a row and the suite stayed green - and no lane compares a human line
/// against its parent commit, then or now, so the register advertised a capability that exists
/// nowhere. Two lanes landed at VM-1 and neither closes that gap; EX-60 records what is left.
/// An over-claim in a register row is the same defect as a rule weaker than its own statement,
/// reached by editing the row instead of the rule.
/// </para>
/// <para>
/// So each row is held to a FIXED EXPECTED TEXT, in all three of its prose fields, and any edit to
/// a group J row - an added sentence, a deleted limit, a reworded claim - fails here and has to be
/// made in both places by someone who has read both. This file is hand-maintained on purpose. It
/// is not generated from the register, because a copy the register produced would agree with the
/// register whatever the register said, which is the tautology rule G was fixed to remove one file
/// over.
/// </para>
/// </remarks>
internal static class AssuranceRegisterRows
{
    /// <summary>The three prose fields of one register row.</summary>
    internal sealed record Row(string Statement, string Evidence, string NonVacuousWhen);

    /// <summary>Every group J row, by identifier.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// J11 is in here with the rest, and it is the row that matters most: it states what the
    /// assurance record must say before a package leaves the component, so a row widened in one edit
    /// widens what may be published.
    /// </remarks>
    internal static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, Row> Expected { get; } = new Dictionary<string, Row>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        ["J1"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """Every relevant unit in the product graph carries a two-line Broiler Code Assurance annotation immediately above its declaration. A code unit is any member that can carry executable code, every field and event field declaration, every enum member, every delegate declaration and every type declaration. Relevance is decided by one declared exemption predicate, `AssuranceScanner.ExemptionFor`, over eight cases plus a per-unit escape hatch; a unit that predicate does not exempt and that carries no parsed block is named, whether the block was never written, written below the declaration instead of above it, or written as half a block. The per-unit `EXEMPT=<reason>` escape hatch may not be used in `Broiler.VM.Binary` at all, and every use of it anywhere is counted and named in `CODE-ASSURANCE.md`. The covered set is every `*.cs` file under a product project directory except the files in that project own `bin` and `obj` directories.""",
            Evidence:
                """The three product assemblies' source, as the generator would leave it, against `AssuranceScanner.ExemptionFor`, `AssuranceScanner.EscapeHatchViolations` and the annotation parser""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """689 relevant units and 903 exempt ones exist across 1,592 code units, so the exemption predicate answers both ways over the checkout and a clean result is a classification rather than a predicate that exempts everything. Every violating witness leaves the assurance comment IN the file, which is what makes the three worth witnessing separately: a coverage check that looked for the text rather than for the attachment would pass all of them. The accepting witnesses carry the other half - units the predicate covers, and one per-unit escape hatch - because a rule that named every unannotated unit regardless of the predicate would be a rule about comments rather than about review. THE LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: the exemption predicate decides relevance, so a unit it wrongly exempts is not reported by this rule. The rule cannot report a unit it was told not to look at. What answers that limit is no longer the predicate alone: rule J7 holds `assurance.manifest.json` to EVERY unit, exempt and relevant alike, and to every covered FILE whole, so a unit this rule cannot see is still watched for change even though nothing reviews it. That is EX-62, narrowed to what it now is. Five cases of the predicate were too broad and were narrowed, each with a witness of its own: case 2 asked only that the right-hand side of an assignment be SOME parameter of the constructor, so every permutation of a constructor body was exempt; case 3 accepted any argument that was a name or a constant, so a delegation supplying the LEB128 width or the budget dimension a charge lands in was exempt and the constant it supplied was the policy; case 1 accepted ANY member access, so `MaxSectionCount => _maxDeclaredCount` was trivial and the same permutation defeat survived one screen below the constructor fix, and case 3 readmitted exactly that shape until it required the same correspondence; case 5 whitelisted logical negation, so every `operator !=` in the component was a delegation to `Equals` when it is the opposite decision; and fields were not units at all, so a `const` budget could be multiplied by sixteen with no annotation to move. The escape-hatch clause is the sixth: replacing all twenty AI lines in `VmBoundedReader.cs` - every unit Security=High - with one sentence referring to nothing left both modes green. `Broiler.VM.Binary` is now closed to the hatch outright and every use elsewhere is counted and named in the component report. What that clause cannot do is judge a reason, which is EX-68. A FOURTH ROUND ATTACKED THE ENUMERATION RATHER THAN THE PREDICATE, and three of its four shapes are answered here, each with a witness: an enum member, which is how the budget dimensions and the reason codes are spelled, is now a unit and is exempt under case 8, because the enum DECLARATION is the unit that carries the vocabulary and its fingerprint covers every member and every value; a type declaration header is now a unit, because round one's permuted-ceiling defeat came back verbatim through a primary constructor, which has no body for case 2 to look at and was in no unit at all; and an event field declaration, the sibling of the field declarations the enumeration already had, is a unit and is deliberately not reached by case 7. A SECOND LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: the covered set is a directory enumeration and not the compiler evaluated `Compile` items, so a file the project pulls in from outside its own directory ships and is examined by nothing in this group. That is EX-63. A THIRD: the enumeration is a whitelist, and the fourth shape of that round - an `[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo]`, which is a member of nothing - can never be a unit however wide the list gets. Rule J7's file fingerprints are what answer that, not this rule."""),
        ["J2"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """Every assurance annotation in the product graph parses as two adjacent comment lines, or three where a falsification criterion stands between them, and every field on the AI line is in its closed vocabulary: `Origin`, `IP` and `Security` from their tables, `Resources` an integer 0 to 10, `Fingerprint` either `TBF` or six uppercase hex characters, `EXEMPT` a non-empty reason stated beside no other field, and nothing the system does not define. An assurance comment that does not parse, or that is attached to no declaration, is reported as stranded rather than ignored. A `Spec` value that cites an ADR names a record that exists under `docs/adr/`. A `// Broiler-Falsified-If:` line parses in that one place, states a criterion, states it on a single line, and carries no `Key=Value` field - it is prose, not data.""",
            Evidence:
                """Every annotation in the three product assemblies against `AssuranceAnnotation.VocabularyProblems` and `AssuranceScanner.OrphanAnnotations`""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """689 annotations are parsed and held to their vocabularies. Each closed vocabulary, each required field and each of the two malformed-exemption shapes carries a witness of its own: one witness with four bad fields would be satisfied by a check that reported the first, and three of the four vocabularies could then be deleted with the suite green. `Spec is optional` and is the only defined field that may be absent, so a rule requiring every defined field would reject the honest annotation on every unit that implements no frozen record. A block that does not parse is reported twice - once for the line that does not parse and once for the human line beneath it, which on its own opens no block - because half a block is the shape a hand-edited annotation leaves. The stranded clause has a witness whose only unit is exempt, so that the input violates this rule and not J1: a block describing a unit that is no longer there is how an annotation outlives its subject, and it is invisible to a coverage rule. The citation clause resolves the ADR NUMBER against the record set on disk and does no more: `Spec=ADR-9999 s42` is rejected because no record 9999 exists, and the section is not checked because the records carry no uniform section index. The falsification criterion carries four clauses with a witness each - an empty criterion, a criterion stating a field, a criterion wrapped onto a second line, and a criterion standing below the human line where the block parses without it - because they are four different ways the line stops being an instruction anybody can act on, and one witness carrying all four would be satisfied by a check that reported the first. The wrapped one is refused at the parse rather than trimmed, so the unit carries no annotation at all and J1 reports it too: a criterion that does not fit on a line is too vague to be one. THE LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: this rule checks that a value is IN its vocabulary and never that it is the RIGHT member of it. An annotation downgraded from `Security=High` to `Security=None` is well formed, and the generated header and the component report are derived from the annotations, so the downgrade is republished faithfully rather than reported as staleness. Nothing mechanical here can tell a correct assessment from a false one; the rubric that would decide it is prose in the component specification and the decision it asks for is a review. That is EX-65. The same limit applies to the criterion one field over, and harder: a criterion that parses, says something on one line and states no field satisfies this rule whatever it says. That is EX-75."""),
        ["J3"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """Every recorded AI fingerprint, as read from disk, equals the one the current declaration produces, and no human fingerprint names a version that is neither the current one nor preserved as `Previous`. The placeholder `TBF` is reported as its own fact, separately from a value that is simply wrong. A per-unit `EXEMPT=` annotation records no fingerprint and is not required to.""",
            Evidence:
                """Every annotated unit in the three product assemblies, scanned from the files as read from disk, against `AssuranceFingerprint.Of` over the declaration's token texts""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """689 annotated units each record a fingerprint the generator filled, and after a generation none records the placeholder - which is asserted, so the clean direction is not satisfied by a tree that has never been generated. The comparison reads the units AS THEY ARE ON DISK. Reading the generator output made this rule vacuous in its clean direction: every `Fingerprint` field there has already been rewritten to the recomputed value, so the rule compared a recomputed value against itself and editing a literal inside an annotated body left it green. Like J5 currency half it therefore runs in gate mode only, because a write run exists to refresh a fingerprint the code has outrun and asserting the pre-write value equalled the post-write value would fail every generation for a reason no reader could act on. The well-formedness assertion beside it reaches only units carrying a full assessment: an `EXEMPT=` line records no fingerprint, the specification documents that, J1 witnesses it being accepted, and applying the assertion to every annotated unit made the documented escape hatch unusable anywhere in the product tree. A `Previous=` fingerprint is accepted, because it is history and not a live approval; reporting it would punish the generator for recording what the policy requires it to record, and would push a reviewer towards deleting the history to get a green suite. The witness fingerprint is baked into the test rather than derived from the witness, because a witness that computed the value it is supposed to record would agree with itself whatever the fingerprinter did; the baked constant is asserted against the fingerprinter in the same test, so a change to normalization fails there and names it rather than quietly making four witnesses vacuous. THE FIRST LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: this rule reaches only ANNOTATED units, which is 689 of the 1,592 in the tree. The other 903 record no fingerprint in any source file and are covered by rule J7's manifest instead, which detects a change to them and reviews nothing. A SECOND LIMIT: six hex characters are 24 bits. The value answers whether this unit changed since it was reviewed and nothing else. It is not a collision-free identifier across units - a component with a few thousand units expects same-value pairs by the birthday bound - and it is not a cryptographic commitment: a party who can choose the code can find a preimage for a 24-bit prefix by brute force in seconds. Detecting a hostile rewrite that preserves the prefix is the job of the git history and the review event, not of this number. That is EX-61."""),
        ["J4"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """No unit is `VERIFIED` and no unit names a reviewer - live, or preserved as `Previous` - unless a human line in the source says so, and no generated artefact carries a human line reading anything but `PENDING`. The generator refuses, by exception, to rewrite a human line outside the four defined shapes at all, and to write any reviewer identifier the line it is reading does not already carry.""",
            Evidence:
                """Every annotated unit and every generated artefact against `AssuranceGenerator.RefuseInventedApproval` and the state machine""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """689 annotated units exist and every one of them reads `PENDING`, which is the honest state of this component and which the rule must not reject. Four generator transitions are witnessed separately - PENDING stays PENDING, an approval that left TBF is filled, a review the code has outrun becomes STALE with its reviewer and its approved version preserved, and a STALE line is not cleared by a recomputation - because the policy's forbidden edge is STALE straight to VERIFIED and one witness would pin only whichever branch fired first. The refusal is an exception rather than a report, so a bug that reached for a name fails loudly instead of quietly manufacturing an approval nobody gave, and it is driven THROUGH `DesiredSource` by a witness of its own rather than only by a direct call: the earlier witness called the guard directly, which pinned the function and not its wiring, so deleting its one call site left the suite green and the guard unreachable during a real generation. The shape clause is the other half of the same repair. A human body of `PENDING; Fingerprint=TBF` named nobody on either side of the name comparison, because the comparison skipped the reserved word wherever it appeared, while the annotation reader wanted exact equality and handed the head token back as a reviewer identifier - so a write run filled the fingerprint, resolved the unit to VERIFIED and published a human review of a line naming no human. Only the exact body `PENDING` now names nobody, and a body outside the four defined shapes is refused before anything is rewritten. The artefact half is asserted on the RENDERED text and not on the model, because a defect that produced the right unit state and the wrong line would be invisible to a check that read only the model - and it takes the artefact list as an argument so that it can be shown REJECTING one. Its only assertion used to be the accepting direction over the real tree, with no rejecting counterpart anywhere, so its whole body could be replaced by an empty list with nothing red. The witnesses carry the identifier WITNESS-ONLY, which appears nowhere outside that directory, because a rule about false approvals cannot be witnessed without an input containing one. THE FIRST LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: this rule refuses a name the tooling produced. It cannot refuse a name a human typed for someone else, or an invented one that reads plausibly - the same limit rule H4 records for the attestation. The policy answers it outside the source, in the pull-request history, and nothing in this component reads that history. A SECOND LIMIT: this rule reads HUMAN LINES, in the source and in the artefacts, and says nothing about the prose around them. A generated header that asserted a review in a sentence rather than on a human line was invisible to it, and rule J9 is what answers that."""),
        ["J5"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """Every generated file header, `CODE-ASSURANCE.md`, `HUMAN_REVIEW.md` and `assurance.manifest.json` is byte-identical to what the generator would produce from the annotations currently in the tree, and the component report's own figures - human reviewed, VERIFIED, unverified, the count of per-unit exemptions and the name of each one, the count of falsification criteria beside the count of units that owe one, and the plain statement that nothing has been reviewed - are true of the unit set it was generated from. Every generated file header states its own file's criteria count beside its security risk. No covered file carries more than one generated assurance block, and no covered file carries a line of the assurance summary vocabulary below the generated header, at any indentation and in any case.""",
            Evidence:
                """The 45 covered product source files, `CODE-ASSURANCE.md`, `HUMAN_REVIEW.md` and `assurance.manifest.json` against `AssuranceGenerator.Plan`, and the report's own rows against `AssuranceSummary.Of`""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """Forty-eight artefacts are in the plan: one per covered product source file, plus the component report, the manifest and the human-review record - which is IN the plan rather than beside it, so the comparison that holds a file header to the tree holds the document a release is refused on. The currency comparison is one function, `AssuranceGenerator.StaleArtefacts`, asserted here and by the generator harness and witnessed by a deliberately stale artefact written to a real file and read back - the clause that gates the checkout previously had no witness at all, because every other J5 witness compares a string the test is holding against its own regeneration. The comparison is byte-identical, because 'equivalent' is what a stale summary always claims to be, and because the policy forbids a hand-maintained figure such as Human-reviewed: 47/47 outright. The currency half runs in gate mode only: under BROILER_ASSURANCE_WRITE=1 the same plan is being applied by the generator harness, and asserting that the pre-write text equals the post-write text would fail every generation for a reason no reader could act on. Every witness runs in both modes, so the rule is never a no-op. The report's figures are checked separately against the units they were computed from, because a byte comparison catches a report that has gone stale and cannot catch a generator that would compute a wrong figure and write it consistently - and the figure that matters most here is the one saying nothing has been reviewed. The per-unit exemption clauses are two, with a witness each - a report that states the count as zero while naming the unit, and a report that states the count and names nothing - because counting a use of the escape hatch and naming it are two different things a report can fail to do, and one witness would pin whichever fired first. One witness covers a hand-written SPDX pair with no generated marker under it, which is the shape every file in this component was in before its first generation and the shape that published the licence declaration twice. The forged-block clauses are stated separately from the byte comparison because a second summary block used to survive regeneration verbatim and become a fixed point, and because the byte difference it now produces does not say what is wrong: the fact a reader needs is that the file carries two summaries and one of them is a forgery. Both halves of that defence were defeated at once by INDENTING the forged block four spaces inside a class body, and a fourth round defeated what remained by WORDING one differently: the recogniser was a literal-string whitelist - exact ordinal equality against the banner and the marker, an ordinal StartsWith against its labels - so a block that shouts its labels, drops the banner and names a person was not a block at all. Both tests are now containment over an assurance summary vocabulary, case-insensitively, on comment lines anywhere below the generated header, and the vocabulary is checked against the product tree: none of its phrases appears in any comment below any generated header, so the clause costs nothing to adopt. The word `verified` is deliberately outside that vocabulary, because `VmVerifiedArtifact` is a public type of this component and its name appears in 112 lines below the generated headers; rule J9 is where that word is held, over the generated text alone. The criteria figures are asserted the same way the review figures are, against the units they were computed from rather than against the text: a header row and two report rows say how much of the high-risk surface carries a falsification criterion, and rule J10 is what names the units behind the shortfall. THE FIRST LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: the generator and the gate are the same code, and two lanes under `.github/workflows/` now compel it - the review lane regenerates every artefact on a pull request and commits what moved, and the publish lane runs the release mode before it packs. What a lane cannot do is what EX-60 is narrowed to: it runs on a pull request and on a tag, so a commit pushed to a branch nobody opens a pull request for is examined by nothing until the next one, and no lane compares a human line against its parent commit to decide whether a transition was legitimate. A SECOND LIMIT: no rule pins the INVOCATION of the currency comparison over the real tree. The function is witnessed and the two call sites both use it, so neither can drift from the other, and a patch deleting both call sites at once removes the gate with nothing red. That is EX-64. A THIRD, AND IT IS WHY RULE J8 EXISTS: this rule compares what is on disk against what the GENERATOR would write, so a sentence the generator itself invents is on both sides of the comparison and cannot be reported here at all."""),
        ["J6"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """No covered product source file contains any preprocessor directive - conditional or not - and the scanner parses every covered file with the conditional symbols the build defines.""",
            Evidence:
                """The 45 covered product source files against `AssuranceSources.DirectiveViolations` over the parse, and `AssuranceSources.ParseOptions`""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """The product tree contains zero preprocessor directives, so the prohibition costs nothing to adopt and the clean direction is a fact about 45 files rather than a vacuous quantifier over none. The rule exists because a directive is TRIVIA, and trivia is what the fingerprint excludes so that a formatter cannot invalidate a review: parsed with no symbol defined, a `#if NET10_0_OR_GREATER` region the build COMPILES becomes disabled text, so a backdoor could be added to an annotated method with its recorded fingerprint unmoved, and a whole public method could ship outside the unit set entirely. That is the fingerprint's central claim - a semantically relevant change must invalidate - failing outright rather than a 24-bit collision. Two defences answer it and they are deliberately independent, each with its own clause and its own witness: the parse now defines a superset of what the SDK defines for net10.0 in both configurations, so a region that ships is scanned, annotated and fingerprinted like any other code and the witness asserts that no disabled text survives the parse; and this rule refuses the directive outright, with one witness for a conditional region - both its opening and its closing directive are named, because a rule reporting only the `#if` would accept a file whose region was opened where it could not see - and one for a `#pragma`, because the rule is about the whole class of directives and not about `#if`. THE LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: this rule reads the same corpus J1 does, so a compiled file outside the scanned set carries any directive it likes and is examined by nothing. That is EX-63. The symbol list is also a list: a target framework added later defines symbols this list does not, and the prohibition is what covers that gap rather than the list."""),
        ["J7"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """`assurance.manifest.json` covers EVERY code unit in the three product assemblies, exempt and relevant alike, each with its fully-qualified name, its file, its exemption state and the fingerprint of its declaration, AND every covered file whole, each with a fingerprint over the complete token stream of its compilation unit: every unit and every covered file present, no entry naming a unit or a file that is not there, and every recorded fingerprint and exemption state the one the current code produces. Nothing in a covered file can change without something moving here, whatever kind of declaration it is. The manifest is a change-detection record, not a review. An entry is not an assessment, not an approval and not evidence that anyone has read the unit; exempt units carry no annotation and no human line, and this rule moves none of them towards being reviewed.""",
            Evidence:
                """`assurance.manifest.json` as read from disk against `AssuranceManifest.Violations` over the units `AssuranceScanner` enumerates and the files `AssuranceSources` covers""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """The manifest covers 1,592 units, of which 903 are exempt and therefore carry no annotation, no fingerprint in any source file and no review state - which is exactly the population this rule exists for - and 45 files, of which the ones declaring no code unit at all are covered by nothing else. Three adversarial rounds returned the same defeat in five different places and every one had one shape: the predicate answered EXEMPT, an exempt unit carries no annotation, an unannotated unit carries no fingerprint, and a unit with no fingerprint has no record of any kind. Narrowing the predicate case by case failed three times because each fix moved the defeat one case over. This rule separates the two questions that were fused: whether a unit needs a human annotation, which the predicate decides, and whether a unit is watched for change, which every unit is. A FOURTH ROUND THEN ATTACKED THE ENUMERATION ITSELF, which is the reason the file fingerprints exist. A unit entry is written only for a declaration kind the scanner names, and four shapes went through that whitelist with the suite green and this file byte-unchanged: an enum member, a type declaration header carrying a primary constructor, an event field declaration, and an `[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo]` that opens every internal type in `Broiler.VM.Runtime`. The first three are units now; the fourth is a member of nothing and can never be one, so a whitelist cannot be completed by adding to it. The six clauses are witnessed separately, because they are six different facts about a checkout and a witness asserted with a bare non-empty check would pin only whichever fired first: a unit present in the tree and missing from the manifest is a unit nothing watches; an entry for a unit that is not in the tree is a record of code that no longer exists; a recorded unit fingerprint that is not the current one is a unit that changed since the manifest was written; a covered file with no file entry is a file nothing watches WHOLE; a recorded file fingerprint that is not current is a file that changed outside every unit; and a file entry naming a file that is not covered is a record of a file that is gone. A seventh witness is the accepting direction over a manifest that covers its tree exactly, so the clean result is a comparison and not a function that reports nothing. An eighth is the assembly attribute itself, read under the tree's own name: the four units and their four fingerprints are asserted to be UNCHANGED by it, so the unit half of the record demonstrably reports nothing, and the file fingerprint is what moves. A ninth asserts that comments are outside the file token stream, which is not decoration: the generator writes a header into every covered file and two annotation lines above every relevant unit, and if either were in the stream the file's fingerprint would depend on the header, the header on the fingerprint, and no generation could ever be a fixed point. THE FIRST LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: detection is not review. A moved fingerprint is a line in a diff and a red suite until someone regenerates; nothing here assesses the change, no human line moves, and a developer may regenerate and accept any change to an exempt unit without anyone reading it. That is EX-67. A SECOND LIMIT: a moved FILE fingerprint says even less than a moved unit fingerprint - it does not say what moved, only that the file is not what it was, and locating the change is a diff a person reads. That is EX-69. A THIRD: the entries carry the same 24-bit fingerprint J3 records, with the same properties and the same non-properties, which is EX-61; and they cover the same directory-enumerated corpus J1 reads, so a compiled file outside that set is in no manifest, which is EX-63. Like J5's currency half, the comparison against the file on disk runs in gate mode only, because a write run exists to refresh it; the witnesses run in both modes."""),
        ["J8"] = new Row(
            Statement:
                """Every generated artefact is line for line the artefact `AssuranceArtefactShape` declares: the manifest's `$comment` block, the sixteen-line generated header of every covered source file, the whole of `CODE-ASSURANCE.md` and the whole of `HUMAN_REVIEW.md`. Fixed text is held to a hand-maintained copy, so the generator cannot invent a sentence; derived content - every count, every portion, every distribution row, the worst assessed IP and Security risk, the list of high-security units, the per-file coverage table and the alias rows - is derived again here and compared by value.""",
            Evidence:
                """`AssuranceManifest.Header`, the generated header of each of the 45 covered source files, the generated `CODE-ASSURANCE.md` and the generated `HUMAN_REVIEW.md`, against `AssuranceArtefactShape`""",
            NonVacuousWhen:
                """Forty-eight pieces of generated text are compared, and each is compared whole rather than by a handful of required phrases: 45 file headers of sixteen lines each, a 21-line manifest header, a component report of about two hundred lines and a human-review record of about three hundred. The record matters most of the four, because its subject is who has read what: a sentence invented in the generator would be a sentence about people, standing in the one place a reader goes to find out. The rule exists because rule J5 structurally cannot report what it reports. J5 compares what is on disk against what the GENERATOR would write, so a sentence the generator itself invents is on both sides of that comparison: two lines appended to `AssuranceManifest.Header` made the manifest assert reviewState=VERIFIED, humanReviewed=true, a named reviewer for every entry and that the component was eligible for release, with both modes of the gate green and every human line in the tree still reading PENDING. The mechanism is the one the group J register rows use - a second copy, kept by hand, compared for equality - and it is deliberately NOT generated from the generator, because a copy the generator produced would agree with the generator whatever the generator said. Where content is DERIVED it is derived again rather than copied, and by a different expression: the IP and Security rows walk their vocabulary from its weakest-claim end and stop at the first value some annotation carries, where `AssuranceSummary.Worst` ranks every annotation and takes a maximum, and the criteria row is counted from the LINE each block records where the generator asks its scanner. That is what pins `AssuranceSummary.Worst`, which feeds the two rows a developer actually reads at the top of every file and which nothing compared against the annotations they summarize. Four clauses have witnesses of their own: the two lines the attack appended to the manifest header, read from a witness and appended to the DECLARED header so the input is the forgery rather than a stale copy of the shape; a file header carrying a row the shape does not declare; a unit set whose three annotations are chosen so that a mistake in either direction changes the answer, with the accepting direction asserting that the generator and the shape agree on it and the rejecting direction asserting that a downgraded Security row is named with both texts; and the high-security list, asserted both by naming every High unit in the real report and by driving a report with one entry removed. The human-review record adds three more: a sentence put into the real record, the coverage table asserted to name every covered file, and the alias rows - which are the claim this document makes about PEOPLE, so the shape rebuilds them from the human lines where the generator asks `AssuranceHumanReview.Reviewers`, and the two are asserted to agree over a witness carrying two aliases, one whose decision the current code answers to and one the code has outrun. That witness is also the only place the multi-alias answer exists at all: nothing in this component names anybody, and the record must still have room for everybody. A further clause answers the limit that discovery exposed: deriving the record twice buys nothing on a branch neither derivation is ever asked about, and this component exercises almost none of them - it has no per-unit exemption, no `STALE` line, no alias, no unit that owes a criterion and carries none, and no covered file that declares nothing. The two derivations are therefore also compared over a synthesized tree carrying all five, with each of those branches asserted to be reached rather than hoped for, so a witness that exercised none of them could not pass by agreeing about nothing. THE FIRST LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: a change made in BOTH places passes. A hand-maintained copy raises the cost of a forgery from one edit to two edits by someone who has read both; it does not make one impossible. That is EX-70, and rule J9 is the independent hold on the same text - it cannot be lifted by editing a shape. A SECOND LIMIT: the report witnesses are the REAL generated report with one line put into it rather than files on disk, because a stored copy of a generated artefact would be a second copy of the thing under test, would go stale at every regeneration, and would be repaired by regenerating it - which is the tautology this register exists to remove. The manifest-header and file-header witnesses are files, so the rule is not witnessed by mutation alone."""),
